WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.641 --> 00:00:09.408
I'm Philip A. Sharp,

00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:11.944
an Institute professor at MIT

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:16.916
in the Koch Institute for Integrative Cancer Research.

00:00:16.917 --> 00:00:18.918
I want to talk about RNA splicing

00:00:18.919 --> 00:00:21.521
and the discovery of split genes,

00:00:21.522 --> 00:00:23.556
but I want to set it in the context

00:00:23.557 --> 00:00:26.292
of the question of "What is a gene?",

00:00:26.293 --> 00:00:29.695
because we use the word gene when we talk to each other,

00:00:29.696 --> 00:00:32.064
but when we start thinking about it

00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:36.769
in the context of specific biochemical structures,

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:38.504
genes are very complicated,

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:41.007
and we really find it very difficult

00:00:41.008 --> 00:00:42.775
to define a gene,

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:44.810
and this can be quite important

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:47.380
in terms of understanding biology.

00:00:47.381 --> 00:00:49.048
Many years ago,

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:52.185
when I began in molecular and cell biology,

00:00:52.186 --> 00:00:55.755
the gene was really quite simple,

00:00:55.756 --> 00:00:57.924
and it's illustrated here

00:00:57.925 --> 00:01:00.026
by a diagram from Jacob and Monod,

00:01:00.027 --> 00:01:03.129
which was published in 1961,

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.665
illustrating the lac operon,

00:01:05.666 --> 00:01:09.602
and the structure of the genes in the lac operon

00:01:09.603 --> 00:01:12.772
and the messenger RNA that comes from those genes,

00:01:12.773 --> 00:01:14.941
and then the proteins you see

00:01:14.942 --> 00:01:17.243
at the bottom of this illustration,

00:01:17.244 --> 00:01:19.946
of galactosidase, permease, and others,

00:01:19.947 --> 00:01:22.882
that are encoded by that messenger RNA.

00:01:22.883 --> 00:01:25.318
And, in 1961,

00:01:25.319 --> 00:01:27.987
this explained what a gene was.

00:01:27.988 --> 00:01:30.990
A gene was, like Z in that diagram,

00:01:30.991 --> 00:01:35.828
a linear sequence encoding and transferring information

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:38.598
into an RNA in a linear fashion,

00:01:38.599 --> 00:01:40.900
and then a protein being coded from it.

00:01:40.901 --> 00:01:43.035
And we even understood

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:45.671
in this year, in 1961,

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:48.040
that there were sequences nearby, operons,

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:49.909
that were cis to the gene

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:51.577
that were critical to the expression of a gene.

00:01:51.578 --> 00:01:55.214
So, in the late 60s/early 70s,

00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:57.383
when I entered cell biology,

00:01:57.384 --> 00:02:00.486
everyone suspected that they understood a gene,

00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:03.322
to the point that Monod actually made the statement

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:06.526
that what is true of bacteria is true of an elephant.

00:02:06.527 --> 00:02:08.728
Well, by and large, that is the case,

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:12.031
but an elephant is a little more complex than a bacteria,

00:02:12.032 --> 00:02:13.900
as we will see.

00:02:13.901 --> 00:02:16.169
Cell biologists have long suspected,

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:20.106
even in the early 70s,

00:02:20.107 --> 00:02:22.441
that genes in mammalian cells

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:24.210
were somewhat more complicated,

00:02:24.211 --> 00:02:26.012
maybe there were mysteries here.

00:02:26.013 --> 00:02:27.980
And this influenced by own thinking

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:30.283
as I moved to Cold Spring Harbor

00:02:30.284 --> 00:02:33.653
to begin to study animal viruses.

00:02:33.654 --> 00:02:36.088
And some of the hints that there might be

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:38.357
a difference in the genetic structure

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:42.161
in mammalian cells, cells with nuclei,

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:45.164
was two general facts.

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:49.135
One is, if you look at the nucleus of a cell,

00:02:49.136 --> 00:02:51.838
you see the outline of nucleus,

00:02:51.839 --> 00:02:54.073
and this is the nucleolus...

00:02:54.074 --> 00:02:56.676
we knew that in the nucleus of a mammalian cell,

00:02:56.677 --> 00:03:00.513
there was a thousand-fold higher levels of DNA.

00:03:00.514 --> 00:03:03.082
It was a thousand-fold more complex

00:03:03.083 --> 00:03:04.784
than in bacteria.

00:03:04.785 --> 00:03:06.819
But it didn't seem that mammalian cells

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:08.888
would need a thousand-fold more genes.

00:03:08.889 --> 00:03:11.624
So we had a lot more DNA in the nucleus of the cell

00:03:11.625 --> 00:03:14.060
than we suspected we needed for genes.

00:03:14.061 --> 00:03:16.696
And then another issue was that

00:03:16.697 --> 00:03:20.366
when we metabolically labeled RNA in the nucleus of a cell,

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:23.169
it was much larger than the RNA

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:26.272
that we found in the cytoplasm of the cell,

00:03:26.273 --> 00:03:28.341
and there was this dichotomy.

00:03:28.342 --> 00:03:30.843
It was larger by a factor of five

00:03:30.844 --> 00:03:33.346
than the size of the message in the cytoplasm.

00:03:33.347 --> 00:03:35.181
So, there was this paradox...

00:03:35.182 --> 00:03:38.484
long nuclear RNA, smaller cytoplasmic RNA...

00:03:38.485 --> 00:03:41.320
the long nuclear had a cap at the 5' end,

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:44.023
and we'll talk a little bit more about that in a moment,

00:03:44.024 --> 00:03:47.960
had polyA at the 3' end, yet it was longer,

00:03:47.961 --> 00:03:49.996
and the message had the same modifications

00:03:49.997 --> 00:03:51.931
and it was shorter.

00:03:51.932 --> 00:03:54.834
So, in 1971,

00:03:54.835 --> 00:03:58.437
I moved to Cold Spring Harbor to start

00:03:58.438 --> 00:04:01.374
learning how to work with animal viruses,

00:04:01.375 --> 00:04:04.110
particularly adenoviruses,

00:04:04.111 --> 00:04:06.979
DNA viruses that caused cancer in animals.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.482
And one of the viruses that we worked with

00:04:09.483 --> 00:04:11.317
was adenovirus 2,

00:04:11.318 --> 00:04:13.986
and this is a micrograph of adenovirus 2.

00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:18.090
It's got a beautiful virus with capsid proteins,

00:04:18.091 --> 00:04:20.393
primarily of the hexon protein,

00:04:20.394 --> 00:04:23.095
that constitutes most of this capsid.

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:26.265
It has its fibers at the end,

00:04:26.266 --> 00:04:29.902
that you see here, that are to attach to cells.

00:04:29.903 --> 00:04:32.171
It's long enough as a genome

00:04:32.172 --> 00:04:34.507
to have 35 genes encoded,

00:04:34.508 --> 00:04:36.709
so it was a wonderful viruses to work with

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:39.645
to probe the cell biology of cells,

00:04:39.646 --> 00:04:42.114
as well as to study its ability

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:44.283
to cause cancer in animals.

00:04:44.284 --> 00:04:46.652
So, we started, in 1970,

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:49.322
at Cold Spring Harbor,

00:04:49.323 --> 00:04:53.059
determining the structure of the genome of adenovirus 2.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:56.696
And that led us to the development of

00:04:56.697 --> 00:04:58.698
restriction endonuclease maps,

00:04:58.699 --> 00:05:01.334
and these are two examples

00:05:01.335 --> 00:05:03.903
of restriction endonuclease maps of the adenovirus genome.

00:05:03.904 --> 00:05:06.772
One is the EcoRI, here at the top,

00:05:06.773 --> 00:05:09.909
and the other is HindIII, at the bottom.

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:11.677
So, we mapped the cleavage sites

00:05:11.678 --> 00:05:14.247
of these restriction enzymes across the genome,

00:05:14.248 --> 00:05:16.682
and therefore were able to start

00:05:16.683 --> 00:05:20.853
preparing a map of the relationship of RNA

00:05:20.854 --> 00:05:22.588
to genome structure.

00:05:22.589 --> 00:05:25.157
And I want you to recall two restriction fragments.

00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:30.096
One is this HindIII 'A' fragment, here,

00:05:30.097 --> 00:05:34.634
and another is this EcoRI fragment, right there.

00:05:34.635 --> 00:05:36.802
And this hexon messenger RNA

00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:38.738
- we knew that the messenger RNA

00:05:38.739 --> 00:05:42.141
mapped across the genome in this position -

00:05:42.142 --> 00:05:44.644
and we wanted to compare the structure

00:05:44.645 --> 00:05:51.017
of the messenger RNA from this hexon protein

00:05:51.018 --> 00:05:52.752
to the nuclear RNA that was expressed

00:05:52.753 --> 00:05:54.187
in the nucleus of the cell,

00:05:54.188 --> 00:05:56.389
and we were going to do this by electron microscopy,

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:59.292
which I had learned at Cold Spring Harbor...

00:05:59.293 --> 00:06:01.727
I mean, at Cal Tech, when I was a postdoc

00:06:01.728 --> 00:06:03.696
with Norman Davidson.

00:06:03.697 --> 00:06:06.098
So, when we did that,

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:09.335
and we in this case is

00:06:09.336 --> 00:06:10.970
Sue Berget and Claire Moore

00:06:10.971 --> 00:06:13.673
- Sue a postdoc, Claire a technician in the lab -

00:06:13.674 --> 00:06:16.576
and we did an R-loop mapping

00:06:16.577 --> 00:06:19.111
between the messenger RNA for hexon

00:06:19.112 --> 00:06:20.780
and the genome.

00:06:20.781 --> 00:06:23.549
What we observed is shown in these series of diagrams.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:25.952
What is shown all the way over here to right

00:06:25.953 --> 00:06:30.590
is the HindIII A fragment in total,

00:06:30.591 --> 00:06:34.794
and then this is a blowup of a piece of that R-loop.

00:06:34.795 --> 00:06:39.198
And what you see here is this section

00:06:39.199 --> 00:06:43.402
of the electron micrograph is the RNA/DNA hybrid.

00:06:43.403 --> 00:06:45.705
And what was puzzling to us was,

00:06:45.706 --> 00:06:49.542
here, a little tail at the 3' of the message.

00:06:49.543 --> 00:06:51.511
And what was really puzzling

00:06:51.512 --> 00:06:54.881
was this little this little tail at the 5' end of the message.

00:06:54.882 --> 00:06:57.783
Now, the 3' end tail, we understood,

00:06:57.784 --> 00:06:59.952
that was the polyA tract,

00:06:59.953 --> 00:07:02.989
but there shouldn't have been a tail at the 5' end of the message.

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:05.958
It should have annealed to the DNA sequences.

00:07:05.959 --> 00:07:07.960
And we spent a large amount of time

00:07:07.961 --> 00:07:10.329
trying to do controls to show

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:13.599
that that tail really didn't come from the DNA sequences,

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:17.003
and therefore that led us to the question of,

00:07:17.004 --> 00:07:19.639
well, where did these sequences come from?

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:22.175
I asked you to remember the EcoRI fragment

00:07:22.176 --> 00:07:24.844
because that allowed us to do

00:07:24.845 --> 00:07:26.946
a very direct experiment

00:07:26.947 --> 00:07:29.782
to test whether the upstream sequences

00:07:29.783 --> 00:07:31.651
in the adenovirus genome

00:07:31.652 --> 00:07:33.920
had the sequences to this tail.

00:07:33.921 --> 00:07:36.822
And what we observed when we made the RNA/DNA hybrid

00:07:36.823 --> 00:07:40.626
between the long fragment and the message for the hexon

00:07:40.627 --> 00:07:43.262
is illustrated here.

00:07:43.263 --> 00:07:46.532
This shows you a diagram of it, here.

00:07:46.533 --> 00:07:49.635
This is the actual micrograph, there.

00:07:49.636 --> 00:07:51.838
And what is striking about this micrograph,

00:07:51.839 --> 00:07:53.873
if you look in this region,

00:07:53.874 --> 00:07:55.942
is there are three loops,

00:07:55.943 --> 00:07:58.444
and that immediately told us that

00:07:58.445 --> 00:08:01.147
that tail had sequences from three different regions

00:08:01.148 --> 00:08:03.916
of the adenovirus genome.

00:08:03.917 --> 00:08:06.085
And we show it here in a diagram

00:08:06.086 --> 00:08:09.388
- an A loop, B loop, and C loop.

00:08:09.389 --> 00:08:12.325
And that told us that the RNA for this hexon message

00:08:12.326 --> 00:08:14.794
was actually being joined together

00:08:14.795 --> 00:08:16.696
out of four different segments,

00:08:16.697 --> 00:08:18.831
and it's diagrammed here in this diagram

00:08:18.832 --> 00:08:21.267
of the RNA/DNA hybrid,

00:08:21.268 --> 00:08:24.070
You can see the hexon RNA message,

00:08:24.071 --> 00:08:26.806
that was the end of that electron micrograph,

00:08:26.807 --> 00:08:29.175
you see the C loop,

00:08:29.176 --> 00:08:32.011
the B loop, and the A loop

00:08:32.012 --> 00:08:34.146
being created by the annealing

00:08:34.147 --> 00:08:36.349
of these sequences off in the genome here,

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:38.985
a', b', c',

00:08:38.986 --> 00:08:41.454
to the hexon message down here.

00:08:41.455 --> 00:08:43.923
And that immediately told us

00:08:43.924 --> 00:08:46.392
that this RNA for the hexon message

00:08:46.393 --> 00:08:49.195
was actually coming from a long RNA

00:08:49.196 --> 00:08:53.366
that was being transcribed from a, b, and c,

00:08:53.367 --> 00:08:55.501
all the way through the genome

00:08:55.502 --> 00:08:57.069
and then spliced together

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:00.606
to link these three segments to the body of the message.

00:09:00.607 --> 00:09:02.808
So, that told us that the secret

00:09:02.809 --> 00:09:04.443
between the long nuclear RNA

00:09:04.444 --> 00:09:06.412
and the shorter cytoplasmic RNA

00:09:06.413 --> 00:09:08.781
was this process of RNA splicing,

00:09:08.782 --> 00:09:11.384
the removal of sequences

00:09:11.385 --> 00:09:14.020
from the middle of the messenger RNA,

00:09:14.021 --> 00:09:16.923
a suggestion no one had made before,

00:09:16.924 --> 00:09:19.859
and it told us, more generally,

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:22.828
that all the genes, or most the genes,

00:09:22.829 --> 00:09:26.032
in these complex mammalian cells

00:09:26.033 --> 00:09:29.302
are expressed by this process of RNA splicing.

00:09:29.303 --> 00:09:32.371
So, in 1977,

00:09:32.372 --> 00:09:35.474
when this work was announced and published,

00:09:35.475 --> 00:09:38.477
we created this diagram that illustrates,

00:09:38.478 --> 00:09:41.180
in the same way that Jacob and Monod did,

00:09:41.181 --> 00:09:44.116
the flow of information from DNA

00:09:44.117 --> 00:09:46.118
into the messenger RNA,

00:09:46.119 --> 00:09:48.321
but in this case in mammalian cells,

00:09:48.322 --> 00:09:51.224
as contrasted to bacterial cells.

00:09:51.225 --> 00:09:53.226
And what you see here, then,

00:09:53.227 --> 00:09:55.328
is the promoter

00:09:55.329 --> 00:09:57.530
where the RNA is initiated,

00:09:57.531 --> 00:09:59.298
the cap is synthesized,

00:09:59.299 --> 00:10:01.734
and from the work of Erin Shatkin and Bernie Moss,

00:10:01.735 --> 00:10:04.770
we knew a great deal about the chemistry of the cap,

00:10:04.771 --> 00:10:08.641
and then over here where there was polyA cleavage,

00:10:08.642 --> 00:10:11.410
cutting of the RNA at a polyA site,

00:10:11.411 --> 00:10:13.379
and a polyA added,

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.615
making a precursor RNA

00:10:15.616 --> 00:10:18.251
that then is spliced

00:10:18.252 --> 00:10:21.454
before the RNA is transported to the cytoplasm, here.

00:10:21.455 --> 00:10:23.856
So, we diagrammed, then,

00:10:23.857 --> 00:10:26.359
the flow of information from DNA

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:29.028
into messenger RNA in mammalian cells.

00:10:29.029 --> 00:10:31.130
Now, this diagram is a little simplified,

00:10:31.131 --> 00:10:34.467
because most genes in mammalian cells

00:10:34.468 --> 00:10:37.703
have ten different introns, not just one,

00:10:37.704 --> 00:10:40.339
and therefore there's much more splicing.

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:43.543
And in many cases that splicing is nascent.

00:10:43.544 --> 00:10:47.079
While the RNA is being synthesized from the DNA...

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:49.749
doesn't occur in a polyadenylated precursor,

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:52.118
but in about one in ten cases

00:10:52.119 --> 00:10:54.620
- one in ten introns -

00:10:54.621 --> 00:10:57.123
it is removed posttranscriptionally,

00:10:57.124 --> 00:11:00.593
with this polyadenylated precursor RNA.

00:11:00.594 --> 00:11:03.162
So, we outlined the pathway

00:11:03.163 --> 00:11:05.131
of the synthesis of messenger RNA

00:11:05.132 --> 00:11:06.699
in higher cells,

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:09.769
and we knew those cells had a much more complex structure.

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:12.138
Now, just for completeness,

00:11:12.139 --> 00:11:13.940
and because no one is interested

00:11:13.941 --> 00:11:16.275
in adenovirus anymore,

00:11:16.276 --> 00:11:18.878
just let me illustrate the complexity

00:11:18.879 --> 00:11:21.013
by which a virus

00:11:21.014 --> 00:11:24.884
uses this process of RNA splicing to express itself.

00:11:24.885 --> 00:11:27.220
You see, as we said before,

00:11:27.221 --> 00:11:30.456
there are three different leaders,

00:11:30.457 --> 00:11:32.291
exons A, B, and C,

00:11:32.292 --> 00:11:34.694
we saw in the previous diagram,

00:11:34.695 --> 00:11:37.363
and those are spliced to all of these messenger RNAs

00:11:37.364 --> 00:11:38.998
across the genome.

00:11:38.999 --> 00:11:41.100
So, there's a long RNA made

00:11:41.101 --> 00:11:43.936
from the left end of the genome across the genome

00:11:43.937 --> 00:11:46.005
and all of these sets of RNAs

00:11:46.006 --> 00:11:47.807
are processed from it.

00:11:47.808 --> 00:11:51.244
And, for example, this is a polyadenylation site

00:11:51.245 --> 00:11:53.312
where the hexon message

00:11:53.313 --> 00:11:55.214
is actually produced by splicing,

00:11:55.215 --> 00:11:58.484
and this is another polyadenylation site

00:11:58.485 --> 00:12:00.786
where another major messenger RNA is used.

00:12:00.787 --> 00:12:03.356
Each of these involves a long precursor

00:12:03.357 --> 00:12:04.824
across the whole genome,

00:12:04.825 --> 00:12:07.360
alternative polyadenylation at different sites,

00:12:07.361 --> 00:12:09.729
and the splicing of those three leaders

00:12:09.730 --> 00:12:11.764
to the end of the message.

00:12:11.765 --> 00:12:14.901
But when we look at the other transcriptional units in the virus,

00:12:14.902 --> 00:12:20.039
here's one, that's the E4, E3, E2, and E1

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:22.041
on the left side, over here,

00:12:22.042 --> 00:12:24.210
you see, again, very complex splicing

00:12:24.211 --> 00:12:26.279
of alternative polyadenylation

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:28.181
and alternative splicing

00:12:28.182 --> 00:12:30.216
accounting for the complexity of the message

00:12:30.217 --> 00:12:32.785
from this adenovirus genome.

00:12:32.786 --> 00:12:36.756
This has led us to think about what is actually a gene,

00:12:36.757 --> 00:12:40.159
and when you start thinking about a gene,

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:42.495
then you ask yourself,

00:12:42.496 --> 00:12:45.031
what's the unit of information in a gene

00:12:45.032 --> 00:12:51.270
that actually accounts for the functional unit of a gene?

00:12:51.271 --> 00:12:53.339
So, this is my favorite gene.

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:55.141
This is from Larry Zipursky,

00:12:55.142 --> 00:12:56.976
who's at UCLA.

00:12:56.977 --> 00:12:58.978
It's the Dscam gene,

00:12:58.979 --> 00:13:02.081
it's expressed in the nervous system of Drosophila,

00:13:02.082 --> 00:13:05.785
and it produces 38,000 different proteins

00:13:05.786 --> 00:13:07.920
from one gene.

00:13:07.921 --> 00:13:10.089
Now, each of these little bars, here,

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:12.325
this is a genomic DNA diagram...

00:13:12.326 --> 00:13:15.828
each of these little bars here is an exon,

00:13:15.829 --> 00:13:19.098
and what you note, here,

00:13:19.099 --> 00:13:22.034
is that there are something like 33 different exons,

00:13:22.035 --> 00:13:24.270
and you can alternatively splice

00:13:24.271 --> 00:13:27.740
one of these into each precursor messenger RNA.

00:13:27.741 --> 00:13:30.543
And this has 48 different exons

00:13:30.544 --> 00:13:32.445
for exon 6

00:13:32.446 --> 00:13:34.313
and 12 for exon 4.

00:13:34.314 --> 00:13:36.382
And these are all used in permutation,

00:13:36.383 --> 00:13:40.319
producing 38,000 different messenger RNAs

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:42.355
and different proteins.

00:13:42.356 --> 00:13:45.825
So, when we talk about this gene,

00:13:45.826 --> 00:13:48.060
is it one of those exons

00:13:48.061 --> 00:13:50.596
being spliced to another to make one functional unit

00:13:50.597 --> 00:13:52.798
that goes into the protein?

00:13:52.799 --> 00:13:54.567
Or is it the whole transcription unit,

00:13:54.568 --> 00:13:56.969
and all 38,000 different proteins,

00:13:56.970 --> 00:13:59.472
with different functions, within the gene?

00:13:59.473 --> 00:14:00.706
And each of those exons

00:14:00.707 --> 00:14:02.842
could potentially be a gene.

00:14:02.843 --> 00:14:06.212
So, this leads us to the question

00:14:06.213 --> 00:14:08.548
that the cellular environment

00:14:08.549 --> 00:14:10.750
can modify the nature of the messenger RNA

00:14:10.751 --> 00:14:13.286
in the transfer of information

00:14:13.287 --> 00:14:15.321
from DNA to protein.

00:14:15.322 --> 00:14:17.056
In one cell,

00:14:17.057 --> 00:14:18.791
the DNA sequence is interpreted one way.

00:14:18.792 --> 00:14:23.429
In another cell, it's interpreted in another way,

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:25.731
through alternative splicing and alternative polyadenylation,

00:14:25.732 --> 00:14:29.168
and something I haven't spoken about, RNA editing.

00:14:29.169 --> 00:14:31.270
So, a given genomic sequence

00:14:31.271 --> 00:14:35.908
is actually not a faithful representation

00:14:35.909 --> 00:14:39.612
of the functional information expressed from a gene,

00:14:39.613 --> 00:14:42.815
and therefore as we look at our genome,

00:14:42.816 --> 00:14:45.818
and now we have the genome sequence,

00:14:45.819 --> 00:14:47.954
it was published in 2003,

00:14:47.955 --> 00:14:50.423
fifty years after Watson and Crick,

00:14:50.424 --> 00:14:54.427
and we say to each other, "We have 23,000 genes."

00:14:54.428 --> 00:14:58.297
Well, we do have 23,000 transcription units,

00:14:58.298 --> 00:15:00.867
which for example accounts for

00:15:00.868 --> 00:15:02.201
where the transcription begins

00:15:02.202 --> 00:15:03.936
on the left side of this diagram

00:15:03.937 --> 00:15:05.905
and moves to the right,

00:15:05.906 --> 00:15:08.174
but you see alternative promoters,

00:15:08.175 --> 00:15:10.176
alternative polyadenylation sites,

00:15:10.177 --> 00:15:13.412
alternative splicing in the middle of the gene, here,

00:15:13.413 --> 00:15:15.381
and you see in some cells

00:15:15.382 --> 00:15:17.850
you would use this information in the gene,

00:15:17.851 --> 00:15:20.253
and in other cells, by alternative splicing,

00:15:20.254 --> 00:15:22.421
you would skip that information.

00:15:22.422 --> 00:15:24.957
So, what's a gene?

00:15:24.958 --> 00:15:28.628
It's a very difficult question to answer.

00:15:28.629 --> 00:15:31.464
When we get to the level of biochemistry,

00:15:31.465 --> 00:15:33.633
are we looking at functional units

00:15:33.634 --> 00:15:36.736
in a genetic transmission sort of thing?

00:15:36.737 --> 00:15:41.140
We use the term gene, among ourselves, all the time,

00:15:41.141 --> 00:15:44.710
but when we start specifically probing a gene,

00:15:44.711 --> 00:15:49.949
the gene is actually a very difficult thing to define.

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:52.585
So, I want to thank you for your attention

00:15:52.586 --> 00:15:55.221
and listening to this discussion

00:15:55.222 --> 00:16:21.615
of what is a gene.

